<title>AUSTRALIA: Australian stocks seen mixed in trendless trade.</title>
<headline>Australian stocks seen mixed in trendless trade.</headline>
<text>
<p>------------------(Snapshot 8.30 a.m./2230 GMT)----------------</p>
<p>AORD cl	    2706.0   -21.5   DJIA cl	8198.45    +4.41</p>
<p>SPI Sycom cl     2721.0    +5.0   US 30yr cl	6.48    +0.03</p>
<p>10yrbond futs    93.535  -0.010   Gold NY 323.90 Lon fix 323.55</p>
<p>AUD   US$0.7405 (Mon: US$0.7390)  CRB Index cl  245.26    +1.90</p>
<p>NIKKEI cl	19668.07 -136.31   FTSE cl	 4895.7    -3.60</p>
<p>S&amp;P500(Globex)   950.30   +3.16   NZSE40	 2558.99    +0.21</p>
<p>--------------------------(Aug 5)------------------------------</p>
<p>*Stocks seen steady to easier, little trend apparent. &quot;It will probably be a bit easier but you may see bargain hunting in some of the stocks hit yesterday,&quot; said a Brisbane broker.  </p>
<p>*Pre-open bids A$17.85 (steady), A$19.37</p>
<p>(steady), A$6.08 (-.01), A$7.68 (-.02)</p>
<p>*Wall Street stocks edged to slender gains as investors</p>
<p>struggled to assess evidence about U.S. economic performance.</p>
<p>*ADRs: News Corp 18-1/8 (-1/4), BHP 26-7/16</p>
<p>(-3/16), WMC 22-7/8 (+1/8), National Australia Bank</p>
<p>72 (-3/8)</p>
<p>*Gold price was weaker, on LME aluminium kept its uptrend,</p>
<p>zinc firmed, copper continued its short term downtrend, while</p>
<p>nickel fell back under profit taking, rest were softer.</p>
<p>-- Sydney Newsroom 61-2 9373-1800</p>
</text>
